course: dictionary
lesson: 30
title: Absurd: silly and not making sense
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Absurd is an adjective. It means very silly, not making sense, so ridiculous that it is almost laughable. You can also say more absurd or most absurd.
explain: In Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, «Alice finds it absurd but keeps a straight face.» Keeping a straight face means trying not to laugh, so something silly is happening. The clue tells you that absurd means silly.
explain: Another sentence says, «Alice thinks it "very absurd" (silly), but they look so grave (serious) that she dares not laugh.» The writer gives the meaning in brackets, and then the clue shows why Alice must not laugh: everyone else looks serious. Look for brackets and clues like these.
explain: In maths, an answer can be absurd too. Minus 3 people, or 2.5 buses, cannot be right, so absurd tells you to check your working. Ask yourself: could this answer happen in real life?
fact: Absurd means very silly and not making sense.
fact: «That's absurd» is a common phrase meaning that is ridiculous.
fact: Something can be absurd when it is silly and funny, or when it is so unreasonable that it is laughable, like «an absurd price».
fact: «The absurd» means silly and senseless things as a whole.
fact: Ridiculous and silly are close in meaning to absurd, so they can often replace it in a sentence.
exercise: «Alice finds it absurd but keeps a straight face.» What does absurd mean here? | Very sad and tearful | *Very silly and not making sense | Very fast and loud | Very kind and generous
exercise: Kai writes that the answer to a sum is minus 3 people. His teacher says this answer is absurd. Why? | *Nobody can have minus 3 people, so the working must be checked | Minus numbers are always wrong in every sum | The answer is too small to matter | People like long answers more than short ones
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to absurd? | *ridiculous | ancient | gentle | ordinary
test: «The shop wanted £500 for one pencil. That's absurd!» What does the speaker think about the price? | It is a bargain worth taking | It is a normal price for a pencil | *It is so unreasonable that it is laughable | It is a kind price for a friend
test: «Alice thinks it "very absurd" (silly), but they look so grave that she dares not laugh.» Why does Alice not laugh? | She is too far away to hear the joke | *Everyone looks serious, so she must not laugh | She has been told to sing instead | She is feeling sad and tired
exam: Which answer to a maths question is absurd? | 3 buses | *2.5 buses | 0 buses | 10 buses
